Commodore Markup Language - CML
===============================

CML is a decades old idea that the existing Internet could easily contain a secondary network specifically designed for Commodore 64 computers.  This network would provide access to the C64's SID and VIC chip with access to text colors, sprites and sounds.

CML is being designed to be hidden within HTML remark code <!-- -->, allowing the code to exist inside of any normal webpage, but not be redendered on the screen of a modern computer.

CML will use it's own CML or simple Commodore Markup Language. 

The CML concept was first announed in 2004 on proboards forums.
https://jledger.proboards.com/board/2/cml

CML is currently back in developement for the Commodore 64 Ultimate computers which
contain internal Swiftlink compatibility.

CML's core swiftlink code is based on the HTTP demo written by Chris Garrett.   
This demo was first shown in the March 2026 Computer Gazette Publication.
The original code can also be downloaded from:
https://github.com/omiq/c64-ultimate/blob/main/http-get.bas

A CML test page is located at http://www.gunstar.one

The entire CML project is in a state of flux as ideas and concepts are being
tested and changes are implemented for testing.

NOTE: REGARDING NEXT PARAGRAH!
CML DOES NOT require Basic Programmer's Toolkit to run and will be perfectly happy in BASIC 2.0

I'm using Basic Programmer's Toolkit for development as it provides some very nice programming aids.

(Note: This software will run on an original C64 with swiftlink device at 1mhz speed)

Recommended Configuration:
==========================
Turbo Settings: U64 Turbo Registers (CML uses Turbo Modes for faster parsing.)
CPU Speed: 1 Mhz
Command Line Interface: Enabled
Enable Software IEC as #9

LOADING AND RUNNING CML:
========================
Place the 'toolkit basic.prg' file in a folder with 'cml.prg'.
Set Software IEC to the folder where these files are located. (F5, 'Set dir. here')
LOAD "CML",9
RUN

